<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Rhodes</h1><p class="st-meta">1204 CE – 1250 CE (approx.) · capital Rhodes</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Independent Byzantine lordship on Rhodes under the Gabalas family after the fall of Constantinople in 1204, later absorbed by the Empire of Nicaea.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> broke up the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> in 1204, the Greek official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-gabalas">Leo Gabalas</a> took control of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes</a> and neighbouring islands and ruled them as his own domain, styling himself Caesar and lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyclades">Cyclades</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-gabalas">Gabalas</a> ruled as an independent Greek lord with a fleet that let him trade and bargain. Under pressure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-doukas-vatatzes">John III Vatatzes</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>, who sent an expedition in 1233, he accepted Nicaean overlordship. In 1234 he made a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> granting its merchants trading rights while securing Venetian support. His brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-gabalas">John Gabalas</a> succeeded him around 1240.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1248 the Genoese seized <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes</a> in a surprise attack. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> recaptured the island around 1250 and ruled it directly as part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/empire-of-nicaea">Empire of Nicaea</a>. In 1310 it fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a>, becoming <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hospitaller-rhodes">Hospitaller Rhodes</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1204 CE – 1250 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.66%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1204 CE – 1250 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rhodes</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Leo Gabalas, John Gabalas</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Medieval Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Rhodes under the Gabalas, Gabalas lordship</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1227&amp;focus=lordship-of-rhodes" aria-label="Lordship of Rhodes on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5390 864 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Rhodes lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5710 1120L5709 970M5710 1120L5730 989"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M5730 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5710 1120h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5710 1120h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Rhodes. <a href="/atlas?year=1227&amp;focus=lordship-of-rhodes">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
